WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:02.769
You have looked at these kinds
of demographic trends,

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:07.240
the way our politics has evolved over
the decades in a really in-depth

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.308
and detailed way.

00:00:08.308 --> 00:00:11.611
And Georgia
is such an interesting example.

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:13.680
I mean, what do you think here?

00:00:14.481 --> 00:00:15.815
Help us understand

00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:19.886
how much of this is a fundamental shift
or a demographic shift versus

00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:23.623
these individual candidates themselves
and of course, the elephant in the room.

00:00:23.656 --> 00:00:24.924
Donald Trump.

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:29.262
Yeah, well, look, every election is
there is a intersection of long term

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:30.730
and near term factor.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:32.432
So all of those figured in.

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:35.235
But to go back to the point
that John Berman finished on.

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:39.873
What we are really seeing
is the impact of the conscious choice

00:00:39.873 --> 00:00:43.343
Republicans have made in the Trump
era to reorient

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:46.379
the party toward the cultural preferences
and grievances

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:52.752
of a predominantly white non-urban
non-college, often evangelical electorate,

00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:58.758
and the impact that has had on suburban
areas that once reliably voted Republican.

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:02.429
I mean, there were five states, Casey,
as you know, that decided the 2020

00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:06.766
election by shifting from Trump in 16
to Biden in 20.

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:09.102
They were Arizona, Georgia,
Michigan, Wisconsin.

00:01:09.169 --> 00:01:10.003
Pennsylvania.

00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:15.008
With Walker's loss
tonight, Republicans have lost all seven

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:18.511
Republicans in those states
who are nominated for governor or senator

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:21.881
with Trump's
specific endorsement have now lost.

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:24.751
And they've lost in a very consistent way

00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:28.054
by having the suburban areas
move sharply against them.

00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:29.589
It wasn't just Gwinnett and Cobb.

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:33.660
It was Oakland in Michigan, Delaware
and Montgomery in Pennsylvania,

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.831
Maricopa
in Arizona and Dane County in Wisconsin.

00:01:37.831 --> 00:01:41.801
So there is a consistent pattern
here of these big populous,

00:01:42.235 --> 00:01:45.071
thriving population centers
in the metro areas,

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:48.141
rejecting the Trump era definition
of the party and their increased

00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:51.644
strength in the rural areas
not being quite enough to overcome that.

00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:54.280
Yeah, no, it's such a good point, Alex.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:54.814
And I mean, I got to,

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:58.952
as the Republican at the table,
bring you in here because the reality is

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:02.155
the people live in our increasingly inner
cities and suburbs.

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:05.992
And the party is increasingly,
as Ron very clearly laid out.

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:09.295
I'm focusing on a message
that appeals to rural voters,

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:14.067
and there are simply not enough of them
to win no national elections.

00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:15.835
And, you know, we're seeing it
in control of the Senate.

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:18.571
There's obviously very narrow control
of the House on the Republican side,

00:02:19.172 --> 00:02:22.442
almost not enough that I'm not even sure
exactly what we can take away

00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:24.344
from the way the House switch this year.

00:02:24.344 --> 00:02:29.015
But I mean, how do
and how can Republicans win back

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:32.252
these voters that they need to win
if they want the White House back?

00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:36.122
Well, they can start with nominating
quality candidates.

00:02:36.456 --> 00:02:39.392
Candidate quality was a big factor
in a lot of these races

00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:43.163
that Ron mentioned, that we lost people
that were flawed candidates

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:46.466
that were backed by Trump
election deniers,

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:50.370
we're okay with the January 6th

00:02:51.037 --> 00:02:54.240
insurrection at the Capitol
that did not sit well with voters.

00:02:54.274 --> 00:02:56.442
So getting quality candidates

00:02:56.442 --> 00:03:00.146
that focus on issues that are important
to voters is really important.

00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:04.951
And this, I think, will go a long way
to what I'm going to say next.

00:03:06.052 --> 00:03:07.220
Ronna McDaniel, the

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:10.290
chairwoman
of the Republican National Committee,

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.660
she was put in that position in 2017.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:15.929
She has been in that position.

00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:19.766
We have Republicans
have not won across the board in any races

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:21.668
since she has been in office.

00:03:21.668 --> 00:03:24.537
I think change starts at the top
in order for Republicans

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:26.105
to do well moving forward.

00:03:26.105 --> 00:03:29.242
Change needs to begin at the top
and therefore

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:33.146
someone like her
in a position of the head of the RNC

00:03:33.413 --> 00:03:38.484
get candidates in the primary process
that can win in the general election.

00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:41.221
This is not about winning
primaries. It's about winning the general.

00:03:41.287 --> 00:03:42.121
I take your point.

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:45.325
But at the end of the day and Paul Begala,
I'm interested in your take on this.

00:03:45.325 --> 00:03:46.960
At the end of the day,

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:50.863
a big part of this is that the best people
in the Republican Party haven't wanted

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:54.567
to put themselves in positions to run
for these offices, to run for Senate.

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:55.835
I mean, we had.

00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:57.904
You mean Chris Sununu in New Hampshire.

00:03:57.904 --> 00:03:58.371
There are

00:03:58.371 --> 00:04:01.574
there were others kind of across the map
that Mitch McConnell really wanted to run

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:05.745
and who didn't run because nobody wanted
anything to do with Trump.

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:07.113
I mean, how do they solve that problem?

00:04:07.113 --> 00:04:09.315
We're not talking
about the Maryland Senate race

00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:11.985
because the incumbent won in a landslide,
the Democrat, because Larry Hogan,

00:04:11.985 --> 00:04:15.955
the incredibly popular Republican governor
of one of the most Democratic states

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:17.490
didn't want anything to do.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.525
Of course,
the politics in the time of Trump

00:04:19.726 --> 00:04:21.561
in the U.S.,
I mean, he's going to run for president.

00:04:21.561 --> 00:04:24.430
But what? And we we wish him well.

00:04:24.631 --> 00:04:27.767
But I think the problem
is not even just geographic.

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:28.034
Right.

00:04:28.034 --> 00:04:31.304
They do have a problem in the cities
and in the suburbs.

00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:36.142
But it's it's what they say,
you know, right after the Civil War.

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:39.279
Henry Adams, a historian and writer
and diplomat, he described

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:43.283
American politics in his day
as the systematic organization of hatreds.

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:46.119
That's Donald Trump's politics.

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:49.656
If he were going to rural America and say,
I'm going to help save rural hospitals,

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:49.956
you know,

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:53.326
one third of all the rural hospitals
in America are under risk of closing.

00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:54.894
That's a crisis.

00:04:54.894 --> 00:04:56.029
There's a crisis on the farm.

00:04:56.029 --> 00:04:57.330
God knows there's an opioid crisis

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.432
in every part of America,
but it's hitting rural America.

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:02.869
If he were going to them with ideas

00:05:02.869 --> 00:05:06.239
and solutions for their very real
problems, there'd be one thing.

00:05:06.406 --> 00:05:07.707
Instead, he's going with grievance.

00:05:07.707 --> 00:05:08.374
Look at Mr.

00:05:08.374 --> 00:05:11.444
Walker running ads attacking trans kids,

00:05:12.145 --> 00:05:16.482
of which I think in high school athletics
in Georgia, there are two and we know

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:20.687
that trans kids have spectacularly higher
suicide ideations and self-harm.

00:05:20.687 --> 00:05:23.690
And that's just it has failed politically.

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:25.591
But I also think morally it's wrong.

00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:27.660
It's better to come to voters
with something

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.296
you'll do for them than just
I hate to say, people you hate.

